      "math_academy_id": "q-75836",
      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "The following table gives the values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(5).$\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $9$ |\n| $5$ | $1$ | $6$ |\n| $8$ | $5$ | $4$ |",
      "worked_solution": "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(5)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(5))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(5)=8$ and ${f}^{′}(8)=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
